WEBVTT

NOTE
Journey: Advocates - Week 3 Leader Video
Unit 1, Lesson 3: Truth

1
00:00:06.900 --> 00:00:11.940
Every year, Oxford Dictionary puts out a word of the year.

2
00:00:12.260 --> 00:00:17.080
The word of the year for 2016 was this,

3
00:00:22.880 --> 00:00:24.940
post-truth.

4
00:00:26.080 --> 00:00:31.840
It's an adjective it's defined as relating to or

5
00:00:31.840 --> 00:00:37.200
denoting circumstances in which objective facts are less influential

6
00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:42.860
in shaping public opinion than appeals to emotion and

7
00:00:42.860 --> 00:00:48.020
personal belief so in other words post-truth means that

8
00:00:48.020 --> 00:00:52.300
you don't really need to worry about objective facts and it's really about how you

9
00:00:52.300 --> 00:00:57.700
feel about something. You don't have to necessarily agree with objective truth,

10
00:00:58.320 --> 00:01:01.760
but your own subjective truth is much more valuable.

11
00:01:02.860 --> 00:01:07.280
Essentially, this is the word of the year and this is exactly what we're up against

12
00:01:07.280 --> 00:01:12.240
or what our students are up against every day that they walk into the classroom at

13
00:01:12.240 --> 00:01:16.800
school, every day that they interact with their friends and they see media and

14
00:01:16.800 --> 00:01:19.880
movies and music and everything else that's on TV.

15
00:01:19.880 --> 00:01:22.000
This is what we're talking about.

16
00:01:22.440 --> 00:01:28.200
It's essentially relativism, the idea that objective truth no longer

17
00:01:28.200 --> 00:01:33.760
matters. It no longer has any space in our heads to navigate our own reality.

18
00:01:34.200 --> 00:01:39.800
In other words, if I don't like objective reality, I can just completely ignore it

19
00:01:39.800 --> 00:01:43.740
and not worry about it and create my own reality, my own truth.

20
00:01:44.180 --> 00:01:49.580
And you can probably already start to imagine what kind of problems this is going

21
00:01:49.580 --> 00:01:54.700
to cause in your own mind against with students, which is also often why we as

22
00:01:54.700 --> 00:01:58.220
leaders will stand in front of our students and start talking about something that

23
00:01:58.220 --> 00:02:01.880
we think and we know is objectively true, especially what's based on Scripture.

24
00:02:02.780 --> 00:02:08.100
And there's a wall between what we're saying and our students,

25
00:02:08.240 --> 00:02:12.380
because oftentimes they're looking at what we're saying and thinking, well, wait a

26
00:02:12.380 --> 00:02:14.760
minute, that's what you think to be true.

27
00:02:14.900 --> 00:02:20.580
I don't accept that as true. Even though we're saying that this is objectively

28
00:02:20.580 --> 00:02:25.520
true, that this is absolutely true, they don't necessarily agree with it.

29
00:02:25.940 --> 00:02:31.900
See, you and I probably accepted truth for what it is, even if we didn't

30
00:02:31.900 --> 00:02:36.460
like it. So okay, this is what truth is, this is the reality of the situation or the

31
00:02:36.460 --> 00:02:38.840
reality of our environment or the reality of our lives.

32
00:02:38.940 --> 00:02:44.280
We may not like it but we accept it and we deal with it or we change it or whatever.

33
00:02:45.500 --> 00:02:51.320
Students will base their truth off of a reality that they create because

34
00:02:51.320 --> 00:02:54.340
they're told that's exactly what you're allowed to do.

35
00:02:56.340 --> 00:03:02.300
But it's important that you help your students see truth for

36
00:03:02.300 --> 00:03:08.000
what it really is. This lesson is so, so important as you continue to move

37
00:03:08.000 --> 00:03:13.880
forward. Because you can't talk about the reality of the, or the authority of

38
00:03:13.880 --> 00:03:16.920
Scripture. You can't talk about the reality of the resurrection.

39
00:03:17.200 --> 00:03:19.200
You can't talk about the existence of God.

40
00:03:19.300 --> 00:03:20.980
You can't talk about the problem of evil.

41
00:03:21.060 --> 00:03:25.400
You can't talk about really anything else that you're going to dive into until your

42
00:03:25.400 --> 00:03:28.380
students understand the nature of truth.

43
00:03:28.720 --> 00:03:32.420
So truth is what corresponds with reality.

44
00:03:32.760 --> 00:03:36.880
When you think of truth and when you help your students think of truth, sort of help

45
00:03:36.880 --> 00:03:42.780
them navigate away from this idea of post-truth and remind them that truth is

46
00:03:42.780 --> 00:03:45.540
what is real. It's what is reality.

47
00:03:46.120 --> 00:03:51.360
So reality is really a thing that's independent of me or you or your students.

48
00:03:51.560 --> 00:03:55.400
It is what it is regardless if we're interacting with it or not, okay?

49
00:03:55.840 --> 00:04:01.320
So more than just understanding truth and reality is the understanding that genuine

50
00:04:01.320 --> 00:04:03.700
freedom comes by living through truth.

51
00:04:03.700 --> 00:04:06.600
Because remember, we're talking about what it means to have faith.

52
00:04:06.720 --> 00:04:08.700
We're talking about truth in that context.

53
00:04:09.020 --> 00:04:10.740
And our students want to be free.

54
00:04:10.780 --> 00:04:13.020
We want them to experience the freedom that is in Christ.

55
00:04:13.160 --> 00:04:15.900
And that comes from understanding what truth is.

56
00:04:16.680 --> 00:04:19.300
And truth is what corresponds with reality.

57
00:04:19.680 --> 00:04:25.500
Now, it might be really tempting for them to think, well, as long as it makes sense

58
00:04:25.500 --> 00:04:28.940
or as long as it's practical, then that's fine.

59
00:04:29.040 --> 00:04:31.320
Then it can be true, right? It can be coherent.

60
00:04:31.480 --> 00:04:32.900
It can be practical. It can be whatever.

61
00:04:32.900 --> 00:04:36.220
As long as it makes sense to me, then it can be true.

62
00:04:36.480 --> 00:04:38.820
Well, that's not necessarily the case.

63
00:04:39.100 --> 00:04:42.760
And if you think about it, there's lots of things that are coherent.

64
00:04:43.300 --> 00:04:48.140
Think about belief in things like Santa Claus or the Easter Bunny or the Tooth

65
00:04:48.140 --> 00:04:52.440
Fairy. When we're kids, those things make all the sense in the world.

66
00:04:52.980 --> 00:04:55.440
But we know these things don't line up with reality.

67
00:04:55.900 --> 00:05:00.760
And parents may spend a lot of time making these things incredibly practical,

68
00:05:00.920 --> 00:05:03.740
but yet they don't line up with reality.

69
00:05:04.560 --> 00:05:09.280
And so it might make sense for some other religious beliefs or some other ideas that

70
00:05:09.280 --> 00:05:14.920
students may come across that sound good, sound like they make sense, sound very

71
00:05:14.920 --> 00:05:18.340
practical. That doesn't mean they line up with reality.

72
00:05:19.220 --> 00:05:24.480
You know, it might sound really nice to say all religions lead to the same place or

73
00:05:24.480 --> 00:05:27.520
that all religions have some idea of truth to them.

74
00:05:27.740 --> 00:05:33.360
But then what do we do in John 14 when Jesus says, I am the way of the truth and the

75
00:05:33.360 --> 00:05:35.920
life. Nobody gets the Father but by me.

76
00:05:36.420 --> 00:05:39.460
Jesus is making an objective truth claim.

77
00:05:39.700 --> 00:05:43.120
So either that is true or it's not.

78
00:05:43.700 --> 00:05:46.200
And it doesn't matter what I think of it.

79
00:05:46.340 --> 00:05:50.720
So either what Jesus said is true or not, whether I interact with it or not,

80
00:05:50.780 --> 00:05:53.940
whether I believe in it or not, it's either true or it's false.

81
00:05:54.100 --> 00:05:55.700
That's what we call objective truth.

82
00:05:56.120 --> 00:06:01.340
So if what Jesus is saying is true, that makes every other belief that

83
00:06:01.340 --> 00:06:05.260
contradicts belief in Jesus to be false.

84
00:06:06.020 --> 00:06:11.280
So we want our students to be able to see that truth is so critically

85
00:06:11.280 --> 00:06:17.120
important to furthering their discussions on what it means to

86
00:06:17.120 --> 00:06:23.040
believe in Christianity so students in small group are going to have a really good

87
00:06:23.040 --> 00:06:28.560
chance this week to discuss and explore how truth opens the door

88
00:06:28.560 --> 00:06:34.160
to a life of freedom because really when we boil this down is

89
00:06:34.160 --> 00:06:37.500
understanding truth is more than just sort of intellectually understanding what

90
00:06:37.500 --> 00:06:42.800
truth is it's understanding it so that it leads them to freedom

91
00:06:42.800 --> 00:06:47.800
that they can be in Christ and they can live the life that God has purposed for

92
00:06:47.800 --> 00:06:52.740
them. So when we're thinking about truth, there's two kinds of truth that we're

93
00:06:52.740 --> 00:06:57.300
really talking about. Now I've talked a lot about the idea of objective truth, but

94
00:06:57.300 --> 00:06:59.240
there's also something we call subjective truth.

95
00:06:59.400 --> 00:07:01.260
So there's objective and subjective.

96
00:07:01.640 --> 00:07:05.280
Well, what's the difference? And oftentimes this is where our view of truth gets

97
00:07:05.280 --> 00:07:08.320
sort of confused because we mix these two up.

98
00:07:08.420 --> 00:07:12.700
So helping to understand these on your end as well as helping your students to

99
00:07:12.700 --> 00:07:16.060
understand this is going to be one of those things that's going to help take that

100
00:07:16.060 --> 00:07:20.840
wall that's dividing you and your students down to help them see exactly what it is

101
00:07:20.840 --> 00:07:22.520
that you're saying when you're talking about Scripture.

102
00:07:22.860 --> 00:07:26.440
So first, let's talk about the idea of subjective truth.

103
00:07:26.660 --> 00:07:29.200
Well, what is the idea of subjective truth?

104
00:07:29.680 --> 00:07:34.840
Well, if I say something like, I love mint chocolate chip ice cream,

105
00:07:34.960 --> 00:07:40.120
okay? But you like another flavor, chocolate, let's say.

106
00:07:40.120 --> 00:07:45.820
Is it true that I think mint chocolate chip ice cream

107
00:07:45.820 --> 00:07:51.540
is the best kind of ice cream well of course it's true because I think it

108
00:07:51.540 --> 00:07:57.120
and is it true at the same time that if you think chocolate ice cream is the best

109
00:07:57.120 --> 00:08:00.680
kind of ice cream that that is in fact a true statement well of course it's a true

110
00:08:00.680 --> 00:08:06.140
statement because you think it see these are subjective truth statements they're

111
00:08:06.140 --> 00:08:11.540
true because they're based on my experience and my experience only

112
00:08:11.540 --> 00:08:17.200
so it doesn't matter and two different things can be both equally true at the same

113
00:08:17.200 --> 00:08:22.020
time two different flavors of ice cream can be both true at the exact same time

114
00:08:22.020 --> 00:08:26.620
because it's completely subjective and if I'm not here to say it then this truth

115
00:08:26.620 --> 00:08:32.260
statement doesn't matter anymore so it's completely subjective on the other side is

116
00:08:32.260 --> 00:08:34.220
an idea of objective truth.

117
00:08:35.360 --> 00:08:39.100
Now, objective truth has to do with the object.

118
00:08:39.320 --> 00:08:44.340
So if you think of it this way, subject, object.

119
00:08:46.680 --> 00:08:49.040
Truth statement is about the subject here.

120
00:08:49.860 --> 00:08:52.600
Truth statement is about the object here.

121
00:08:53.180 --> 00:08:58.960
Okay? So if I'm talking about ice cream, I'm talking about how I

122
00:08:58.960 --> 00:09:04.840
feel about the ice cream but here if i'm talking about something like my shirt if

123
00:09:06.500 --> 00:09:12.340
i told you that my shirt was blue you tell me i'm wrong well why because my shirt

124
00:09:12.340 --> 00:09:18.160
isn't blue my shirt is green until my shirt is black or white or anything but the

125
00:09:18.160 --> 00:09:23.360
color that my shirt actually is that i'm wrong it's completely independent of my

126
00:09:23.360 --> 00:09:25.400
opinion it's completely independent of my experience.

127
00:09:25.760 --> 00:09:30.940
Even if I never bought the shirt and it's still on the rack at the store, the shirt

128
00:09:30.940 --> 00:09:35.080
is still the color that it is until it ceases to be that shirt in that color.

129
00:09:35.620 --> 00:09:41.400
So it doesn't matter. So it's based on the object, my shirt, not my opinion.

130
00:09:41.560 --> 00:09:44.400
This is based on my opinion. I'm the subject.

131
00:09:44.880 --> 00:09:46.900
So that's kind of the distinguishing feature.

132
00:09:47.160 --> 00:09:52.500
The problem is, is that what we do is we lump religion over here

133
00:09:52.500 --> 00:09:58.420
and we lump Jesus on the side of subjective truth and

134
00:09:58.420 --> 00:10:03.600
we say that well I can believe in Jesus subjectively because based on my experience

135
00:10:03.600 --> 00:10:09.400
I think it's true and it doesn't matter what anybody else thinks and it doesn't

136
00:10:09.400 --> 00:10:12.660
matter if you disagree because somebody else can believe in something completely

137
00:10:12.660 --> 00:10:17.660
different and it'd be equally true but now we go back to John 14.

138
00:10:18.980 --> 00:10:22.780
Jesus said something that is independent of me.

139
00:10:22.960 --> 00:10:27.560
He said something that is an objective truth statement, whether it's true or not.

140
00:10:27.740 --> 00:10:31.540
So that puts belief in Jesus on the objective side.

141
00:10:31.840 --> 00:10:35.340
So when we're talking about Jesus, we're talking about Christianity.

142
00:10:35.760 --> 00:10:39.540
We're talking about the way in which the world works and God being creator and all

143
00:10:39.540 --> 00:10:41.560
the things that we're going to talk about for the rest of the year.

144
00:10:41.660 --> 00:10:44.240
We're talking about objective truth statements.

145
00:10:44.460 --> 00:10:46.860
They're either true or they're not.

146
00:10:47.440 --> 00:10:50.360
And we have to make sure our students understand that they're here.

147
00:10:50.680 --> 00:10:55.040
It's not a matter of opinion or experience, it's a matter of reality.

148
00:10:55.600 --> 00:11:01.220
So truth is what corresponds with reality, which then leads

149
00:11:01.220 --> 00:11:03.560
to freedom.

150
00:11:04.360 --> 00:11:09.820
This is what we want our students to see primarily this week, is that understanding

151
00:11:09.820 --> 00:11:14.180
objective truth, understanding that truth is often objective, understanding that

152
00:11:14.180 --> 00:11:15.840
belief in Jesus is objective.

153
00:11:16.740 --> 00:11:21.660
It is how it corresponds and lines up with reality, and understanding those things

154
00:11:21.660 --> 00:11:26.260
is what's gonna lead your students to the feeling and the understanding and the

155
00:11:26.260 --> 00:11:29.220
experience of genuine freedom in Christ.

